{
"content_id": "14409",
"reference_id": 13577,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "3 John 1:14–15",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "64001015",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<ul><li><p>John closes his letter with his farewell and with greetings to others.</p></li><li><p><em>Peace to you</em>: This was a traditional Jewish greeting (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"40010012\" data-end-ref=\"40010012\">Matthew 10:12</data>; <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"42010005\" data-end-ref=\"42010006\">Luke 10:5–6</data>). It became more meaningful for Christians because Jesus used it after his resurrection (<data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"43020019\" data-end-ref=\"43020021\">John 20:19–21</data>, <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"43020026\" data-end-ref=\"43020026\">26</data>).</p></li><li><p><span><em>Greet each of our friends there by name</em></span>: John expected Gaius to greet the friends. This meant those who accept the good news about Jesus taught by the apostles and live according to the truth (compare <data class=\"bible-ref\" data-start-ref=\"64001003\" data-end-ref=\"64001004\">3&nbsp;John 1:3–4</data>).</p></li></ul>",
"associations": {
